{"text": "Background\n==========\n\nMammalian sperm acquire most of their unique characteristics through their own evolutionary history as sperm inseminated females. Consequently, all species of the mammalian clade have evolved specialized spermatogenesis, which enables each species to develop and maintain a unique combination of sperm production parameters and sperm traits with respect to the function of each species. The evolutionary history of sperm production has been the subject of an increasing number of research efforts. This progress includes the identification of sperm types and genes that produce these sperm, their molecular evolution, as well as the evolutionary changes that they undergo during their reproductive career.\n\nThe evolutionary changes that sperm undergo before fertilizing an egg are mostly based on sperm\\'s own properties, such as sperm movement, motility, size, and shape. These morphological characteristics are under evolutionary selection to ensure that sperm are capable of fertilizing viable eggs within the female reproductive tract (sperm competition). The shape of the sperm is especially important, because shape is thought to be one of the most important determinants of sperm motility (egg penetration), where shape affects the trajectory of the sperm during the encounter with the egg. However, the evolutionary changes that sperm undergo throughout their lifetime are generally poorly understood.\n\nThe evolution of sperm motility\n------------------------------\n\nIn general, the evolution of sperm motility is associated with a series of evolutionary shifts that allow sperm to achieve its maximal energy potential. Motile sperm have evolved several strategies to increase their energy utilization. To enable high-speed movement (as required by the female reproductive tract), sperm have developed mechanisms that increase their propulsive velocity by promoting the formation of numerous long, thin filaments of actin (Fleming and Shine, [@B35]). Because actin polymerization is enhanced by the activation of sperm enzymes, sperm are able to produce an explosive increase in sperm motility (Fleming and Shine, [@B35]).\n\nThe evolutionary shift to increased high-speed motility involves three key evolutionary steps. First, sperm have acquired a unique combination of actin-based motors. The actin-based sperm motility machinery consists of actin filaments and proteins (including proteins involved in sperm motility, the sperm surface, and the acrosomal matrix). Second, the sperm motility machinery has evolved to include numerous sperm-specific actin-based motors. Sperm-specific actin-based motors are sperm-specific actin-based proteins that are found only in the sperm population (Bisgaard et al., [@B12]; Bredenkamp and Schoeler, [@B16]; Hahn et al., [@", "depth": 1, "seed_id": 1, "token_count": 512}
